{
  "dom_id": "question-167390",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "The acceleration $a(t)$ of the particle is given by the derivative of the velocity with respect to time. Find the function $a(t).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡t$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we write\n\n$$\nv(t)=3{csc}^{2}⁡(t).\n$$\n\nNow, we see that $v(t)$ can be expressed as the composite function\n\n$$\nv=3{u}^{2},\\,u=\\operatorname{csc}⁡t.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}=6u,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & ={v}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =6u\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{csc}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{csc}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t) \\\\  & =-6{csc}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
